* ============================================================================
* 01_dbf_to_csv_v2.dts   --  DBF -> CSV, runnable
*
* Replaces 01_dbf_to_csv_students.dts, which could not execute:
*   EXPORT CSV <f> HEADER ON DELIMITER , DATES ISO QUOTES RFC4180
*     -> EXPORT grammar is EXPORT [TO] <file> [CSV|PIPE]. DELIMITER, DATES and
*        QUOTES options do not exist.
*   _drops/  -> does not exist under the runtime data root; TMP does.
*   USE students -> ambiguous: no students.dbf at the DBF root; four variants
*        live in sandbox/ og/ x64/ with row counts 215/200/210/204.
*
* Fixture is built here from the SEALED Cascade package so the test never
* depends on an ambiguous pre-existing table. See README_SUITE_REPAIR_V1.md.
* ============================================================================

SET TALK ON
ECHO CONV01 BEGIN dbf-to-csv

* -- build a known fixture from sealed, checksummed source ---------------------
ECHO CONV01 FIXTURE autodbf from sealed Items.csv
AUTODBF X64 CONVERSION_FIXTURE_ITEMS_V1 FROM cascade_precision_erp/data/Items.csv HEADER INFER OVERWRITE

* -- export it back out --------------------------------------------------------
ECHO CONV01 EXPORT
USE CONVERSION_FIXTURE_ITEMS_V1
COUNT
EXPORT TO tmp/conv01_items_export.csv CSV

* -- prove the export is readable by re-importing it ---------------------------
* A file being written is not proof it is well formed. Round-tripping it through
* AUTODBF proves the CSV is parseable and preserves shape.
ECHO CONV01 REIMPORT
AUTODBF X64 CONVERSION_FIXTURE_ITEMS_REIMPORT_V1 FROM tmp/conv01_items_export.csv HEADER INFER OVERWRITE
USE CONVERSION_FIXTURE_ITEMS_REIMPORT_V1
COUNT
STRUCT

ECHO CONV01 END

* ============================================================================
* PASS CRITERIA
*   fixture COUNT            = 18
*   reimport COUNT           = 18      <- export lost no rows
*   reimport field count     = 13      <- export lost no columns
*   tmp/conv01_items_export.csv exists and has a header row
*
* NOTE: EXPORT writes a header row by default (per its contract notes), which is
* why the reimport can use HEADER. Type letters after reimport may differ from
* the fixture where EXPORT rendered a value differently than the sealed CSV --
* any such difference is a REAL FINDING about export fidelity, not a test bug.
* Record it rather than adjusting the expectation.
* ============================================================================
